    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The difference between a public adjuster vs insurance adjuster comes down to who hires them and who pays them. An insurance adjuster works for your insurance company. A public adjuster works for you, the policyholder, and is paid a share of your claim settlement. Both can be skilled and honest, but they sit on opposite sides of the table.</p>
<p>This guide explains the three types of adjusters you may meet after a Texas property loss, how public adjusters are regulated and paid, and how to decide whether hiring one makes sense for your claim.</p>

<h2>The three types of adjusters</h2>
<p>When you file a homeowners or rental property claim, you may deal with one or more of these professionals.</p>
<h3>Staff adjusters</h3>
<p>A staff adjuster is an employee of your insurance company. They inspect damage, review your policy, write estimates and recommend how much to pay. Many routine claims are handled entirely by staff adjusters, sometimes by phone or from photos you upload.</p>
<h3>Independent adjusters</h3>
<p>An independent adjuster is a contractor hired by the insurance company, often through an adjusting firm. Insurers bring them in when claim volume spikes, such as after a hurricane or a major hailstorm, or when they do not have staff in an area. An independent adjuster is not independent of the insurer in the sense of being neutral. The insurer assigns and pays them, and they report to the insurer.</p>
<h3>Public adjusters</h3>
<p>A public adjuster, called a public insurance adjuster in Texas law, is hired by the policyholder. They inspect the damage, read your policy, prepare a detailed estimate and inventory, and negotiate with your insurer on your behalf. They are paid by you, usually as a percentage of the claim settlement.</p>

<h2>Public adjuster vs insurance adjuster: side-by-side comparison</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th></th><th>Staff adjuster</th><th>Independent adjuster</th><th>Public adjuster</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who hires them</td><td>Insurance company (employee)</td><td>Insurance company (contractor)</td><td>Policyholder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who pays them</td><td>Insurance company salary</td><td>Insurance company fee</td><td>You, usually a percentage of the settlement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost to you</td><td>None directly</td><td>None directly</td><td>Up to 10% of the settlement in Texas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Whose interests they represent</td><td>The insurer</td><td>The insurer</td><td>The policyholder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Texas license</td><td>Issued by TDI for those who regularly adjust claims</td><td>Adjuster license issued by TDI</td><td>Public insurance adjuster license issued by TDI</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Most routine claims</td><td>Catastrophes and high claim volume</td><td>Large, complex or disputed claims</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>One point that surprises homeowners: a good insurance adjuster is supposed to pay what the policy owes, no more and no less. Hiring a public adjuster does not change what your policy covers. It changes who is building the case for how much is owed.</p>

<h2>How Texas licenses and regulates public adjusters</h2>
<p>Public adjusters in Texas must be licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) under Chapter 4102 of the Insurance Code. TDI's consumer guidance and the law set out several protections worth knowing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Written contract required.</strong> The fee and terms must be in a written contract. TDI publishes a sample contract form.</li>
<li><strong>72-hour cancellation.</strong> You can cancel the contract by written notice within 72 hours after signing, for any reason. After that, you generally cannot get out of it.</li>
<li><strong>No contractor work.</strong> A public adjuster cannot act as your contractor or take part in repairing the property they are adjusting. Contractors also cannot advertise that they will handle your insurance claim.</li>
<li><strong>Solicitation limits.</strong> TDI says public adjusters cannot knock on doors soliciting business during a natural disaster or after 9 p.m.</li>
<li><strong>No legal advice.</strong> Public adjusters cannot practice law or give legal advice.</li>
<li><strong>No referral fees.</strong> They cannot accept referral fees from attorneys, contractors or anyone else involved in your claim.</li>
<li><strong>You stay in control.</strong> A public adjuster cannot keep you from talking with your insurance company, and the insurer's check must name you as a payee. A public adjuster may not sign or endorse your claim check for you.</li>
</ul>
<p>TDI's page on <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/tips/public-adjusters.html">what to know before you hire a public adjuster</a> covers these rules in plain language.</p>

<h2>The Texas public adjuster fee cap</h2>
<p>Texas law caps a public adjuster's total compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement on the claim. The fee can be a percentage, an hourly rate, a flat amount or another method, but the total cannot exceed that cap.</p>
<p>Two details matter when you compare offers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The percentage can apply to the whole payment.</strong> TDI gives this example: if the insurer pays $100,000 and only $20,000 was in dispute, a 10% fee could still be $10,000. Ask whether the fee applies to the full settlement or only to amounts above the insurer's original offer.</li>
<li><strong>Quick policy-limit payments.</strong> If the insurer pays, or commits in writing to pay, the policy limit within 72 hours after the loss is reported, the public adjuster generally cannot take a percentage fee. They can be paid reasonable compensation for time and expenses instead.</li>
</ul>
<p>You still owe your deductible, and if the insurer does not increase its offer, you may still owe the adjuster's fee. Before signing, run the math: estimate the likely increase, subtract the fee, and see what is left. Our <a href="/tools/insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator/">insurance claim depreciation calculator</a> can help you see how much of the current offer is depreciation that may be recoverable anyway.</p>

<h2>When hiring a public adjuster makes sense</h2>
<p>A public adjuster tends to add the most value when the claim is large, complicated or stuck. Situations where it is worth getting a consultation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Major losses.</strong> A house fire or serious hurricane damage can involve hundreds of line items, structural repairs, code upgrades and a long contents inventory. See our guide on <a href="/blog/what-to-do-after-a-house-fire/">what to do after a house fire</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple coverages.</strong> Claims that involve dwelling, other structures, contents and loss of use at once.</li>
<li><strong>Wind vs water disputes.</strong> When the insurer attributes damage to an excluded cause, such as flood or wear and tear. Our <a href="/blog/hurricane-damage-insurance-claim-texas/">Texas hurricane claim guide</a> explains why this split matters.</li>
<li><strong>A lowball estimate you cannot rebut.</strong> If the adjuster's numbers are far below contractor bids and you do not have the time or expertise to argue line by line.</li>
<li><strong>Business or rental property.</strong> Investors with lost rent or several damaged units often benefit from someone managing the paperwork.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When a public adjuster probably isn't worth it</h2>
<p>A public adjuster is not right for every claim. You may be better off handling it yourself when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The claim is small and the cause of damage is clear.</li>
<li>Your insurer has already offered a fair amount that matches your contractor's estimate.</li>
<li>The insurer paid or committed to pay your policy limits quickly.</li>
<li>The dispute is about whether something is covered at all, which may be a legal question for a Texas attorney rather than an estimating question.</li>
<li>Your policy has an appraisal clause and the disagreement is only about price. Appraisal may resolve it without a percentage fee.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Questions to ask in a consultation</h3>
<p>Most public adjusters offer a free first meeting. Use it to compare candidates, not to sign on the spot.</p>
<ul>
<li>Is your fee a percentage, hourly or flat, and does it apply to the full settlement or only to the increase?</li>
<li>Will you personally inspect the property, or will someone else?</li>
<li>How many claims like mine have you handled, and can I speak with past clients?</li>
<li>How will you keep me updated, and how long do you expect the claim to take?</li>
<li>What happens to your fee if the insurer does not raise its offer?</li>
</ul>
<p>Many homeowners get a fair result by documenting carefully, getting their own contractor estimate, asking for a re-inspection and keeping a written record of every conversation. Good records help no matter who handles the claim.</p>
[[product:document-scanner]]

<h2>Red flags and how to verify a license</h2>
<p>Most public adjusters are professionals who follow the rules. But disasters attract bad actors, so watch for these warning signs:</p>
<ul>
<li>Knocking on doors during a disaster or late at night.</li>
<li>Pressure to sign the same day, or a contract with blank spaces.</li>
<li>Promises of a specific settlement amount before inspecting.</li>
<li>Offers to also do the repairs, or to waive your deductible.</li>
<li>Telling you not to speak with your insurance company.</li>
<li>Asking you to sign over your claim check or payment rights.</li>
<li>Wearing a badge or implying they work for the government or your insurer.</li>
</ul>
<p>To verify someone before you sign:</p>
<ol>
<li>Ask for their full legal name and Texas license number.</li>
<li>Call the TDI Help Line at 800-252-3439 or use TDI's online license search to confirm an active public insurance adjuster license.</li>
<li>Ask TDI about complaints and any disciplinary actions.</li>
<li>Check reviews and ask for references from past clients with similar claims.</li>
<li>Read the full contract, confirm the fee terms, and keep a signed copy.</li>
</ol>
<p>If a claim involves a coverage denial or a possible lawsuit, it is worth confirming your options with a licensed Texas attorney as well.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Staff and independent adjusters work for your insurer. A public adjuster works for you and, in Texas, can charge up to 10% of the settlement. For a large or disputed loss, a licensed public adjuster can be worth the fee. For a small, clear-cut claim, careful documentation and your own estimate may get you the same result at no cost. Either way, verify the license with TDI, read the contract, and remember you have 72 hours to cancel. Find more help in our <a href="/category/insurance-claims/">insurance claims</a> guides.</p>
